This test attempts to verify that SQL queries executed inside (and outside) of
stored procedures end up being normalized and added to the fingerprints system
table, along with their cost metrics.

"t10.req" verifies that the comdb2_fingerprints systable also exposes
bufferpool page-in counters (total_sql_pagein_read, total_sql_pagein_read_io) for a
fingerprint. Since the exact page counts depend on bufferpool cache state and
are not deterministic across environments, the test checks structural
invariants instead of exact values: total_sql_pagein_read is at least the
execution count, and total_sql_pagein_read_io never exceeds total_sql_pagein_read.

"t11.req" checks the same page-in invariants for two distinct SQL statements
executed inside a single stored procedure (Lua inner statements), verifying
each gets its own independently-tracked page-in counters rather than one
statement's counters leaking into the other's.

"t12.req" (driven by "fpwrite.sh") verifies the write-side counterpart: writes
are applied on the master, which attributes the page-in I/O it generates to the
originating statement's fingerprint (comdb2_fingerprints.total_write_pagein_read
/ total_write_pagein_read_io). This relies on the "osql_send_fingerprint"
tunable, enabled in lrl.options, which has the replicant transmit the
fingerprint to the master. Because writes are applied on the master -- which
need not be the node the client connected to -- the test looks the fingerprint
up on the connected node (where the query text exists) and then asserts its
write counters on the master by fingerprint hash. As with t10/t11, only
structural invariants are checked (write page-ins were attributed, and the
disk-I/O subset never exceeds the total), since exact counts are not
deterministic.

"t13.req" (driven by "fpwrite2.sh") verifies the same master write-apply
attribution for the non-SOSQL isolation levels -- read committed, snapshot
isolation, and serial -- which buffer writes in shadow tables and drain them to
the master at commit. serial is enabled via "enable_serial_isolation" in
lrl.options (which also brings up the snapshot machinery); the levels are then
selected per transaction with "set transaction ...". For each level it runs an
UPDATE and DELETE against pre-existing (committed) rows and asserts those
fingerprints carry write page-ins on the master. Rows updated/deleted must be
pre-existing: deleting a row inserted in the same transaction cancels in the
shadow table and never reaches the master.

"t14.req" (driven by "fpapply.sh") carries the attribution one hop further, to
the replicants. With "log_fingerprint 1" in lrl.options the master writes the
fingerprint it received into the transaction log, and every node applying that
log attributes its page-ins to it -- reported separately from the master's own
write-apply counters, as total_replication_pagein_read. The test asserts those
counters on a non-master node, again structurally only, after giving
replication a bounded window to catch up. A single-node run has nothing
applying a replication stream, so the test is vacuously satisfied there and
emits the same line.

-------------------------------- SPECIAL NOTES --------------------------------

The "t03.req" test file purposely excludes the following fingerprints from its
result set (i.e. via the WHERE clause):

    4f16a8ec9db90f803e406659938b2602: "SELECT 1" ==> "SELECT?;"
    69d1558c950bbb908f4b8df0d63e2cf4: "SELECT comdb2_host();"
    ba65a98635122c91120d37676cbfc1ef: "EXEC PROCEDURE sys.cmd.send();"

The corresponding SQL statements are excluded because these are (sometimes?)
used by the test suite itself at various stages.  Since the exact quantity
of these SQL statements is not deterministic, they must be excluded.
